  "final_message": "The incident center is built: a Flask API under `/api` plus a React and Tailwind frontend that the same server serves. I checked it in two ways:\n\n- **Backend:** I ran the account and incident flows through Flask's test client. That covered register and login, opening, resolving and reopening an incident, timeline posts, search and filters, and public stats.\n- **Served app:** I ran it under gunicorn and confirmed `/api/health`, `/api/public/stats` and the frontend page load, and that `/api/incidents` answers 401 without a token.\n\nI did not drive the web pages in a browser (the frontend only passed `tsc` and `vite build`), and I did not build the Docker image.\n\n- **Auth:** Registration needs a 3\u201332 character username (letters, digits, `.`, `_`, `-`) and a password of at least 8 characters. Usernames are case-insensitive. Login tokens are stored hashed and last 7 days. All `/api/incidents*` routes need a bearer token, and `/api/public/stats` is open.\n- **Incident status:** The statuses are `investigating`, `identified`, `monitoring` and `resolved`. You didn't specify them, so these are my choice.\n- **Reopening:** Moving an incident out of `resolved` clears `resolved_at` and adds one to `reopen_count`.\n- **Timeline posts:** A posted event takes a `message` and an optional `type`, which defaults to `note`. Posting `status_change` as a type is rejected.\n- **Validation errors:** These come back as 400 with `{\"error\": ..., \"fields\": {...}}`, and the open-incident form shows each field's message.\n- **Frontend routes:** `/` is the public page, `/login` and `/register` are the account forms, `/incidents` is the list, `/incidents/new` is the open form, and `/incidents/{id}` is the incident page.\n- **Tooling:** I added Tailwind, TypeScript and the type packages to `frontend/package.json`, and updated the lock file. `npm run build` now runs `tsc` first. I also added a `.gitignore` and a `frontend/tsconfig.json`.\n\nThe SQLite database is `data/incidents.db`, created on startup.",
